<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Check the page source, read the missing section and then come back.</p>
<p><span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span></p>
<p>I am gonna spoil this but here's why she never questions the right thing, or tries to escape. The Karcist who trained her was originally more in line with the general sarkic beliefs until that devotion cost her her relationship with her daughter. Since she loved her daughter more than the road she was on, she changed to try to be a better person. It didn't work, but the Karcist never gave up and keep trying, until we get to Sarah.</p>
<p>Sarah isn't a perfect person. What she is, is heavily indoctrinated to an ideology. And while it is moral, her surrogate mother figure did impress it upon her from age six on. Like Virgil mentioned in the final interview, she can't say <em>no</em> to her morality and personal ethics, even if it's not good for her. Healing people severely weakens her. Everything she does is because of this Karcist's attempts at atonement.</p>
</div>
