<p>Hello! Thank you for pointing out the obvious: I am not Leonardo Da Vinci. That's a good thing to get out of the way. It would be really weird if my art was on par with like, one of the Renaissance masters because I probably wouldn't be on this site if it was.</p>
<p>From a meta-perspective, this was included because I encountered a lot of people who wondered how her appendages worked. Here's the answer. As for the sexualization, my resources for figure models are limited, so I used some old pics of myself as reference.</p>
<p>In-universe, while they dehumanize SCPs <em>in documentation</em> they still know they're humans. So including nude photographs would be iffy, in my mind.</p>
<p>I could have drawn this in any style, and I could have easily taken it to a much more artistic level, (I've been doing this for years.) But I choose to depict 4197 in a somewhat sexual position and at this level of skill, to make the reader see that that's how Doctor Turner sees her. The definition of the body vs the lack of it on the appendages is meant to give you the idea that he's focused on her normal parts. When you get to the interviews, He says a lot that he admires what she did, and he intervenes when she's in her depressed state. There's a decent amount of rapport and care in his lines to her, because Doctor Turner is attracted to her, and the picture is supposed to reflect that. Plus he's in charge of this document, so its his call what picture goes up.</p>

