			<div class="content" id="post-content-4440745">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Thank you for pointing out the obvious: I am not Leonardo Da Vinci. That's a good thing to get out of the way. It would be really weird if my art was on par with like, one of the Renaissance masters because I probably wouldn't be on this site if it was.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I wasn't trying to be derogatory, I was pointing out that it is very jarring to see an amateur drawing in what is otherwise &quot;scientific&quot; fiction. I obviously don't expect you to be da Vinci, the same way I don't expect PhD level physics in a gravitational SCP, but if said gravitational article has wonky basic physics, then it's better to not have it at all since it hurts the article. Having a sketch here is the same, the lack of it would be better. <a href="https://66.media.tumblr.com/93a434122040a70939a0f58e15a83c58/fb6a472ebda1a8c8-b6/s1280x1920/407cec390c478ce41d682dbf7715823b41687c05.jpg">Here's a better example of what is seen in scientific drawings.</a> It is a &quot;sketchy&quot; style, but there is a lot of detail and precision in order to get it as close to the truth as possible.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>From a meta-perspective, this was included because I encountered a lot of people who wondered how her appendages worked. Here's the answer. As for the sexualization, my resources for figure models are limited, so I used some old pics as reference.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You could have simply put it on a comment as an out of universe reference. You did hide part of the story in the source code, after all. We wouldn't find that unless we read the comments.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In-universe, while they dehumanize SCPs in documentation they still know they're humans. So including nude photographs would be iffy, in my mind.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>An understandable view, but even without it being a photograph, she's still sexualized. Real medical pictures aren't actually sexualized, they feel very neutral. Kinda sterile, in a way.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But I choose to depict 4197 in a somewhat sexual position and at this level of skill, to make the reader see that that's how Doctor Turner sees her. The definition of the body vs the lack of it on the appendages is meant to give you the idea that he's focused on her normal parts. When you get to the interviews, He says a lot that he admires what she did, and he intervenes when she's in her depressed state. There's a decent amount of rapport and care in his lines to her, because Doctor Turner is attracted to her, and the picture is supposed to reflect that. Plus he's in charge of this document, so its his call what picture goes up.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That came to mind after I read the interviews, but it still feels like it doesn't work. The in-universe reason seems weird because more than just Dr. Turner would see the page, such as Dr. Virgil, and having a sexualized drawing of the SCP would easily raise several eyebrows, and would most likely be reported.</p>
<p>The meta problem is that the concept isn't really explored. Take <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-847">SCP-847</a> or the <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-0166">rewrite of SCP-166.</a> Both of those explore the sexualization of female characters both in writing itself, as well as the in-universe aspects, in depth as analysis of the inherent problem about that trope, but here it doesn't go past basic character interactions. Sure, Dr. Turner likes 4197, but there isn't much of a follow up on how that affects her containment, his job, or the general rules of the Foundation. It feels too superficial for it to excuse a common problem found in media.</p>
<p>I really like this bit of UraniumEmpire's comment in his SCP-166 rewrite (and his comment in general, really):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There's points to be made about the diegetic implications of a piece, and how that can break something in two. Such points, however, must concede that they are extensions of the author's will. Anything put to paper was put there through the direction of a creator.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's true that you wrote a reason for the drawing to be there, but <em>you</em> chose to add the drawing, not the character since he isn't real. So whatever the reason in-universe is, this piece still has an image of an objectified woman, which I believed should be properly explored in the article for it to be there. Otherwise it's the same as the &quot;she chose to dress slutty&quot; argument.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
