															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drradium" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5145684); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5145684&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608238224" alt="DrRadium" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5145684)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/drradium" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5145684); return false;" >DrRadium</a></span> <span class="odate time_1585313255 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Mar 2020 12:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4567511">
									

<p>Like cybersqyd, I liked the concept of portraying the people involved with sarkicism in a deeper and more nuanced manner and less &quot;always chaotic evil&quot;. And like cybersqyd I have some issues with the dialogue, just different ones.</p>
<p>I'm not bothered by the fact that it was almost certain that she was going to take up the offer eventually. Nor was I bothered by the healing hero aspect. I'm not a surgeon but I am a physician so I've known many and plenty of surgeons see themselves this way. And actually the dialogue read a lot like what I might expect from an idealistic med student, and that is my issue.</p>
<p>If she was a surgeon, she went through not only med school but a mentally and physically grueling and often frankly abusive residency. Possibly a fellowship on top of it.</p>
<p>Classic med school joke (mild profanity)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>What's the difference between a medical student and dog shit? Nobody goes out of their way to step on dog shit.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>And surgery has an unfortunately deserved reputation of being sexist and especially abusive and hard on women. SCP-4197 went through all that and thrived. But she often reads like an med student needing reassurance. She meekly inquires about how Dr Turner is doing when she there should be undertones of &quot;of course Marcus is going to be OK.&quot; She might be arrogant and dismissive of others or she could be gracious and even humble. Both are consistent with being a surgeon. But no matter what, she should radiate confidence in her abilities.</p>
<p>Even so, I liked it enough to give it +1 as is.</p>

							</div>

								
					
